msgid ""
"Using the hint above, explain why the result we got for the QFT of "
"$|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} (|0\\rangle + |N/2\\rangle)$ is "
"expected.\n"
msgstr ""
"En utilisant l'indice ci-dessus, explique pourquoi le résultat qu'on a "
"obtenu pour la QFT de $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} (|0\\rangle + "
"|N/2\\rangle)$ est attendu.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Réponse\">\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The original state has a relative phase of 0 (or an integer multiple of "
"$2\\pi$) between the two parts of the superposition. So, we know this state "
"has Fourier components whose phases also match up in that way: the ones that"
" have 0 phase shift between the |0000> term and the |1000> term. Each "
"Fourier basis state $|\\phi_y\\rangle$ is composed of terms whose phase "
"accumulates at a rate of $2\\pi y/N$, meaning, when ordered in the usual "
"way, each term in the superposition has a phase of $2\\pi y/N$ greater than "
"the term that came before. So, at the halfway point $N/2$, we want the phase"
" $2\\pi y/N * N/2$ to be an integer multiple of $2\\pi$. This happens when "
"$y$ is even.\n"
msgstr ""
"L'état original a une phase relative de 0 (ou un multiple entier de $2\\pi$)"
" entre les deux parties de la superposition. On sait donc que cet état a des"
" composantes de Fourier dont les phases s'accordent de cette façon : celles "
"qui ont un déphasage nul entre le terme |0000> et le terme |1000>. Chaque "
"état de base de Fourier $|\\phi_y\\rangle$ est composé de termes dont la "
"phase s'accumule à un taux de $2\\pi y/N$, ce qui signifie que, ordonnés de "
"la façon habituelle, chaque terme de la superposition a une phase de $2\\pi "
"y/N$ supérieure au terme précédent. Donc, au point médian $N/2$, on veut que"
" la phase $2\\pi y/N * N/2$ soit un multiple entier de $2\\pi$. Cela se "
"produit quand $y$ est pair.\n"

